1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:02,310
Saturday, the 1st of February,

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:05,370
2003, 854 in the morning,

3
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:09,770
Eastern Time, a console officer in Mission Control leans into his microphone.

4
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:15,090
He has just watched four temperature sensors on the left wing go dead at the same instant.

5
00:00:15,430 --> 00:00:17,690
He says the thing you were trained never to have to say.

6
00:00:18,110 --> 00:00:21,470
There is no commonality. Four minutes later,

7
00:00:21,470 --> 00:00:25,310
the flight deck answers a routine call and the answer stops in the middle of a word.

8
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:28,910
The report prints it exactly as it came down,

9
00:00:29,450 --> 00:00:32,250
Roger, and then, in square brackets,

10
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:36,210
cut off in mid -word. Seven people were aboard.

11
00:00:36,830 --> 00:00:39,610
One of them was a 41 -year -old flight engineer from Carnall,

12
00:00:40,070 --> 00:00:43,390
in Haryana, who had chosen her own name on her first day of school.

13
00:00:44,870 --> 00:00:47,070
This is what the 16 days actually looked like,

14
00:00:47,350 --> 00:00:49,370
and what was already wrong when they got there.

15
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:55,390
Mind -wired, Columbia, the 16 days,

16
00:00:57,070 --> 00:01:00,870
Carnall, Haryana, 1600 kilometers from the sea,

17
00:01:01,290 --> 00:01:03,510
and a long way from anywhere that launched rockets.

18
00:01:05,090 --> 00:01:08,690
Her father had come across the border at partition with nothing and built a business

19
00:01:08,690 --> 00:01:11,390
out of it. By the time his youngest was at school,

20
00:01:11,530 --> 00:01:12,510
the family was comfortable.

21
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:14,790
This is not a story about poverty.

22
00:01:14,790 --> 00:01:19,790
It is a story about what a girl in 1960s Haryana was allowed

23
00:01:19,790 --> 00:01:23,530
to want. Her mother is the reason any of it happened.

24
00:01:24,090 --> 00:01:25,970
She put all four of her children through school,

25
00:01:26,650 --> 00:01:30,450
daughters included, at a time and in a place where educating a girl was considered a

26
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:31,910
luxury nobody needed to afford.

27
00:01:33,270 --> 00:01:35,230
The youngest had not been formally named.

28
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:37,230
At home, they called her by a nickname,

29
00:01:37,570 --> 00:01:39,010
so on the day she was enrolled,

30
00:01:39,350 --> 00:01:41,710
the principal asked the child what she wanted to be called.

31
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:45,610
She chose Kalpana, it means imagination.

32
00:01:47,270 --> 00:01:48,190
Think about what that means.

33
00:01:48,930 --> 00:01:52,530
Before she could read, before anyone had decided anything else about her life,

34
00:01:52,890 --> 00:01:54,750
the first decision on the record was hers.

35
00:01:56,190 --> 00:01:58,350
Her father took her to the flying club at Carnall.

36
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:01,070
There is film of it, a hangar,

37
00:02:01,490 --> 00:02:05,590
a yellow aircraft, a girl who would not stop asking questions about how the air moved

38
00:02:05,590 --> 00:02:10,070
over the wing. And then, when she said that was what she wanted to do,

39
00:02:10,470 --> 00:02:11,710
almost everyone told her no,

40
00:02:12,410 --> 00:02:13,990
her father, her teachers.

41
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:15,930
There was nothing in it for girls.

42
00:02:17,430 --> 00:02:21,270
She went to Punjab Engineering College anyway and took a degree in aeronautical engineering

43
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:26,470
in 1982. Then the University of Texas for a master's in

44
00:02:26,470 --> 00:02:30,930
1984. Then Colorado for a doctorate in 1988.

45
00:02:32,510 --> 00:02:34,450
That same year she started at NASA Ames,

46
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:36,510
her research was fluid dynamics,

47
00:02:37,130 --> 00:02:40,550
specifically the way air behaves around a wing close to the ground.

48
00:02:42,050 --> 00:02:43,930
Her research at Ames was on powered lift,

49
00:02:44,270 --> 00:02:48,610
how air behaves around an aircraft flying close to the ground where the flow comes back

50
00:02:48,610 --> 00:02:50,270
off the surface and changes everything.

51
00:02:51,610 --> 00:02:55,630
In 1993 she left for a company in Los Altos and became its vice president,

52
00:02:56,030 --> 00:02:58,710
working on how to simulate several moving bodies at once.

53
00:03:00,150 --> 00:03:03,070
She spent years of her life modeling how air moves over a wing.

54
00:03:03,810 --> 00:03:08,870
Remember that. In December 1994 NASA picked its 15th group

55
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:10,910
of astronauts, 23 people.

56
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:16,710
She was one of them. She had become an American citizen two years earlier in 1991.

57
00:03:18,170 --> 00:03:20,290
Her first flight was November 1997.

58
00:03:21,110 --> 00:03:23,190
She was the prime operator of the robotic arm,

59
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:26,730
252 orbits, 6 .5 million miles.

60
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:29,690
Asked what it was like the first time,

61
00:03:30,050 --> 00:03:34,610
she said that when you look at the piece of land,

62
00:03:35,010 --> 00:03:36,590
you feel like you are from the solar system.

63
00:03:38,030 --> 00:03:40,810
And on that flight, something went wrong that she was blamed for.

64
00:03:42,210 --> 00:03:45,810
A satellite called Spartan was released by the arm and failed to turn as it should.

65
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:47,670
She tried to grapple it again,

66
00:03:47,970 --> 00:03:49,350
got no clear capture signal,

67
00:03:49,610 --> 00:03:52,270
and moved the arm back. The satellite began to spin.

68
00:03:53,670 --> 00:03:56,430
Two of her crewmates had to go outside and catch it by hand.

69
00:03:57,030 --> 00:03:58,570
The instrument never did its science.

70
00:03:59,010 --> 00:04:00,550
The first finding was crew error.

71
00:04:01,710 --> 00:04:05,250
Five months later NASA finished its investigation and cleared her.

72
00:04:05,670 --> 00:04:07,470
The failure was in the software interfaces,

73
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:10,590
and in the procedures the crew and the ground had been given.

74
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:13,630
Six years before a piece of foam,

75
00:04:14,110 --> 00:04:18,010
Kalpana Chala was personally blamed for something a review later found was built into

76
00:04:18,010 --> 00:04:21,110
the process. Then she flew again.

77
00:04:22,450 --> 00:04:24,390
One more thing from her NASA file.

78
00:04:24,930 --> 00:04:26,670
She held a flight instructor's license,

79
00:04:26,670 --> 00:04:28,690
rated for aeroplanes and gliders,

80
00:04:29,370 --> 00:04:30,990
commercial licenses for single engine,

81
00:04:31,330 --> 00:04:33,310
multi -engine, seaplanes and gliders,

82
00:04:33,830 --> 00:04:36,510
and instrument rating. She flew aerobatics for fun.

83
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:41,610
The girl they told there was nothing in flying for her ended up licensed to teach other

84
00:04:41,610 --> 00:04:46,010
people how to do it. Across both flights she spent 30 days,

85
00:04:46,550 --> 00:04:48,870
14 hours, and 54 minutes off the Earth.

86
00:04:50,470 --> 00:04:52,990
The 16th of January, 2003.

87
00:04:54,430 --> 00:04:57,470
Columbia lifts off at 29 minutes past 10 in the morning.

88
00:04:58,290 --> 00:05:02,890
The mission had been planned for 2001 and pushed back 13 times before it flew.

89
00:05:04,250 --> 00:05:05,810
It is not going to the space station.

90
00:05:06,430 --> 00:05:08,310
There is no docking and no rendezvous.

91
00:05:08,850 --> 00:05:11,290
Columbia is carrying a laboratory in its payload bay,

92
00:05:11,570 --> 00:05:14,210
and the whole point of the flight is what happens inside it.

93
00:05:15,370 --> 00:05:17,650
Around 80 experiments run around the clock.

94
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:21,170
To do that the crew splits in two and the orbiter never sleeps.

95
00:05:22,270 --> 00:05:26,870
To understand why that mattered you have to go back 22 years to the first time a space

96
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:29,610
shuttle ever flew. April,

97
00:05:29,910 --> 00:05:32,730
1981. Columbia is the first orbiter,

98
00:05:32,950 --> 00:05:34,530
and this is the first flight of the program.

99
00:05:34,930 --> 00:05:36,510
It comes back with damaged tiles.

100
00:05:37,910 --> 00:05:42,870
The fuel tank is covered in insulating foam because it holds liquid hydrogen at 253 degrees

101
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:45,190
below zero and would otherwise ice over.

102
00:05:46,230 --> 00:05:48,150
The design requirement was explicit.

103
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:50,470
The tank was not supposed to shed anything,

104
00:05:50,930 --> 00:05:52,950
not foam, not ice, nothing.

105
00:05:54,370 --> 00:05:55,870
It shed foam on the first flight,

106
00:05:56,490 --> 00:05:59,790
and on the second, and it kept doing it for 22 years,

107
00:06:00,270 --> 00:06:02,330
on flight after flight after flight.

108
00:06:03,490 --> 00:06:06,790
There is a particular place on the tank called the left bipod ramp.

109
00:06:07,510 --> 00:06:08,910
Before Columbia's last flight,

110
00:06:09,310 --> 00:06:11,870
foam had come off that exact ramp at least six times.

111
00:06:13,270 --> 00:06:15,730
At the flight readiness review before Columbia launched,

112
00:06:15,730 --> 00:06:17,710
managers were told there had been three.

113
00:06:19,130 --> 00:06:20,850
Two of the six had not been found yet.

114
00:06:21,510 --> 00:06:22,870
The board discovered them afterwards,

115
00:06:23,230 --> 00:06:24,290
going back through the record.

116
00:06:25,090 --> 00:06:28,390
The people approving that flight were working from a number that was wrong by half.

117
00:06:29,630 --> 00:06:35,030
In December, 1988, Atlantis came back from a classified mission with more than 700 damaged

118
00:06:35,030 --> 00:06:36,770
tiles and one missing altogether.

119
00:06:38,290 --> 00:06:40,930
The commander later said he believed they were going to die on reentry.

120
00:06:41,530 --> 00:06:44,230
The damage sat directly beneath a steel antenna plate,

121
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:46,650
and that is the only reason the wing survived.

122
00:06:48,330 --> 00:06:49,590
There is a word for what was happening,

123
00:06:49,950 --> 00:06:51,490
and the accident board used it.

124
00:06:52,210 --> 00:06:53,850
NASA had two categories for problems,

125
00:06:54,450 --> 00:06:56,370
in -family and out -of -family.

126
00:06:57,770 --> 00:06:59,690
In -family meant something you had seen before.

127
00:07:00,330 --> 00:07:02,850
Foam loss had been seen so many times that it had become,

128
00:07:03,210 --> 00:07:04,910
by definition, in -family.

129
00:07:05,650 --> 00:07:09,510
And by definition, problems in the lesser category got less attention.

130
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:13,330
Red Shift. Rick Husband, the commander,

131
00:07:13,830 --> 00:07:16,850
45. Kalpana Chala, flight engineer,

132
00:07:17,370 --> 00:07:20,630
41. Laurel Clark, a Navy flight surgeon,

133
00:07:20,990 --> 00:07:26,130
41. And Ilhan Ramon, 48, a colonel in the Israeli Air Force and the first

134
00:07:26,130 --> 00:07:29,110
Israeli ever to fly. Blue Shift.

135
00:07:29,730 --> 00:07:31,070
William McCool, the pilot,

136
00:07:31,470 --> 00:07:34,250
41. Michael Anderson, payload commander,

137
00:07:34,530 --> 00:07:39,930
43. David Brown, 46, a Navy flight surgeon who was also a naval aviator.

138
00:07:41,710 --> 00:07:45,010
Before he left, Husband recorded a videotape for each of his two children.

139
00:07:45,590 --> 00:07:47,430
One message for every day he would be gone.

140
00:07:48,030 --> 00:07:49,310
Scripture, a short reading,

141
00:07:49,690 --> 00:07:53,850
something encouraging. He had prayed with them at bedtime every night.

142
00:07:54,350 --> 00:07:56,190
The tapes were how he kept doing that from orbit.

143
00:07:57,650 --> 00:08:02,350
On the first day, David Brown pointed a camcorder out of the window and filmed the external

144
00:08:02,350 --> 00:08:07,690
tank falling away. Michael Anderson photographed the same moment with a 400mm lens.

145
00:08:09,210 --> 00:08:11,630
Brown sent 35 seconds of that film down to the ground.

146
00:08:12,110 --> 00:08:16,010
In those 35 seconds, the part of the tank everyone would later care about had already

147
00:08:16,010 --> 00:08:21,090
rotated out of shot. The accident board went back and looked at how cruiser trained to

148
00:08:21,090 --> 00:08:25,210
film a tank separation and concluded he almost certainly had more of it than he sent.

149
00:08:26,630 --> 00:08:27,830
Nobody asked him for the rest.

150
00:08:28,390 --> 00:08:31,810
That is one of eight moments the board would later list as an opportunity that was there

151
00:08:31,810 --> 00:08:36,810
and was not taken. Brown was the one who had filmed it and the one nobody asked.

152
00:08:37,310 --> 00:08:39,070
He had spent the flight documenting everything.

153
00:08:39,610 --> 00:08:41,390
He was the crew's unofficial cameraman.

154
00:08:42,790 --> 00:08:44,490
35 seconds went down to the ground.

155
00:08:45,110 --> 00:08:47,530
Whatever else was on that tape came down with the orbiter.

156
00:08:49,130 --> 00:08:50,770
Chala's work on this flight was combustion.

157
00:08:51,370 --> 00:08:54,210
How fire behaves when there is no up for the flame to go.

158
00:08:55,550 --> 00:08:58,030
Without gravity, a flame does not make a teardrop.

159
00:08:58,430 --> 00:09:01,970
It makes a sphere. This is the real footage from inside that rig.

160
00:09:03,530 --> 00:09:07,490
The woman who had spent a decade modeling how air moves over a wing spent her last two

161
00:09:07,490 --> 00:09:09,610
weeks watching how fire moves without it.

162
00:09:10,690 --> 00:09:12,130
Anderson was the payload commander.

163
00:09:12,870 --> 00:09:17,350
Every experiment on board was ultimately his responsibility and he had flown once before

164
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:21,110
to Mir. He had grown up on Air Force bases,

165
00:09:21,510 --> 00:09:26,070
the son of an airman, and he was one of very few black astronauts NASA had ever flown.

166
00:09:27,410 --> 00:09:29,110
Ramon's experiment looked the other way,

167
00:09:29,430 --> 00:09:33,510
down. He was photographing dust storms over the Mediterranean and the Middle East to

168
00:09:33,510 --> 00:09:34,810
measure how they changed the climate.

169
00:09:36,270 --> 00:09:37,570
He had carried something with him,

170
00:09:38,130 --> 00:09:42,230
a copy of a pencil drawing called Moon Landscape of the Earth as seen from the moon.

171
00:09:43,610 --> 00:09:49,090
It was drawn in 1942 by a 14 -year -old boy in the Terezin Stadt ghetto from imagination.

172
00:09:49,710 --> 00:09:51,110
His name was Peh Turjins.

173
00:09:51,690 --> 00:09:53,770
He was murdered at Auschwitz two years later.

174
00:09:54,890 --> 00:09:57,750
Yad Vashem made Ramon a facsimile to take up.

175
00:09:58,210 --> 00:09:59,650
The original never left Jerusalem.

176
00:10:00,110 --> 00:10:03,550
It is still there. Ramon was not religious,

177
00:10:03,930 --> 00:10:06,870
but he carried the drawing because of what his own family had survived.

178
00:10:07,370 --> 00:10:09,490
His mother and his grandmother came out of Auschwitz.

179
00:10:11,090 --> 00:10:15,810
He said he saw himself as representing every Jew and every Israeli who never got the

180
00:10:15,810 --> 00:10:19,250
chance. Laurel Clark ran the life sciences.

181
00:10:20,090 --> 00:10:21,970
Spiders, silkworms, ants,

182
00:10:22,450 --> 00:10:26,370
fish, and experiments designed by schoolchildren in six countries who wanted to know

183
00:10:26,370 --> 00:10:27,790
what their animals would do up there.

184
00:10:29,350 --> 00:10:31,550
Some of those experiments survived what happened next.

185
00:10:32,130 --> 00:10:34,790
A canister of roundworms was found alive in the wreckage.

186
00:10:36,550 --> 00:10:37,750
Clark wrote home during the flight.

187
00:10:38,270 --> 00:10:42,490
She described watching a sunrise from orbit and how the smell of a rose grown on board

188
00:10:42,490 --> 00:10:47,890
had changed in space. She signed off saying she hoped everyone was doing well and

189
00:10:47,890 --> 00:10:48,990
that she would see them soon.

190
00:10:50,050 --> 00:10:52,030
And William McCool, the pilot,

191
00:10:52,370 --> 00:10:54,850
was on his first flight. Of the seven,

192
00:10:55,250 --> 00:10:57,190
he was the one who had never been to space before.

193
00:10:58,770 --> 00:11:02,390
Brown had been a gymnast and worked a circus as an acrobat before he went to medical

194
00:11:02,390 --> 00:11:07,010
school. He qualified as a naval flight surgeon and then talked his way into flight training.

195
00:11:08,490 --> 00:11:12,050
Anderson had told an interviewer before the flight that he knew the risks and that he

196
00:11:12,050 --> 00:11:15,010
was at peace with them. While they worked,

197
00:11:15,390 --> 00:11:17,490
something was already being decided on the ground.

198
00:11:18,850 --> 00:11:20,670
81 .7 seconds after liftoff,

199
00:11:21,330 --> 00:11:23,570
a piece of insulating foam came off the fuel tank.

200
00:11:24,210 --> 00:11:25,210
Two -tenths of a second later,

201
00:11:25,210 --> 00:11:26,770
it hit the underside of the left wing.

202
00:11:28,150 --> 00:11:30,670
Nobody saw it happen. Not in the control room,

203
00:11:31,010 --> 00:11:35,990
not on the pad. It was found the next day in the routine review of the launch film.

204
00:11:37,390 --> 00:11:39,290
And when they went looking for a clear picture of it,

205
00:11:39,570 --> 00:11:43,030
they did not have one. Of the ground cameras that should have filmed the ascent,

206
00:11:43,690 --> 00:11:47,790
one lost the vehicle, one was out of focus because of a badly maintained lens,

207
00:11:48,050 --> 00:11:49,970
and one caught only the top of the wing.

208
00:11:51,110 --> 00:11:53,530
The board added a single sentence to that finding.

209
00:11:54,290 --> 00:11:56,430
Camera problems also hindered the Challenger investigation.

210
00:11:57,970 --> 00:11:59,290
So over the next five days,

211
00:11:59,630 --> 00:12:01,850
three different people asked for a photograph of that wing.

212
00:12:03,130 --> 00:12:04,830
The first was on flight day two.

213
00:12:05,610 --> 00:12:07,970
Bob Page, who chaired the photo working group,

214
00:12:08,270 --> 00:12:11,170
walked up to a shuttle program manager and asked in person.

215
00:12:12,350 --> 00:12:14,750
The second and third came on flight day six,

216
00:12:15,150 --> 00:12:16,610
a contractor manager by phone,

217
00:12:17,070 --> 00:12:19,670
and Rodney Rocha, the chief engineer for the heat shield,

218
00:12:19,910 --> 00:12:24,210
by email. Rocha's email is worth reading for one word in it.

219
00:12:24,570 --> 00:12:26,550
Can we petition? And then in brackets,

220
00:12:27,150 --> 00:12:29,010
beg for outside agency assistance.

221
00:12:30,330 --> 00:12:31,970
On the morning of the 22nd,

222
00:12:32,150 --> 00:12:34,870
the American military started work on taking that photograph.

223
00:12:35,630 --> 00:12:37,070
Ninety minutes later, it was called off.

224
00:12:38,110 --> 00:12:42,550
The reason recorded was that NASA had its own resources and no longer needed the help.

225
00:12:44,130 --> 00:12:46,990
The engineers were left doing the only thing still available to them,

226
00:12:47,370 --> 00:12:50,130
trying to calculate the answer instead of photographing it.

227
00:12:50,990 --> 00:12:52,990
The tool was a model called Crater,

228
00:12:53,370 --> 00:12:55,850
built and tested for debris about three cubic inches,

229
00:12:56,450 --> 00:13:00,130
ice, mostly. The piece that hit Columbia was,

230
00:13:00,550 --> 00:13:01,870
by the board's later estimate,

231
00:13:02,270 --> 00:13:05,930
around 400 times bigger than anything that model had ever been tested against.

232
00:13:07,070 --> 00:13:09,410
And here is the part almost everyone gets wrong.

233
00:13:09,950 --> 00:13:11,710
Management did not overrule the engineers.

234
00:13:12,350 --> 00:13:16,170
The engineers talked themselves down on reasoning that was defensible and turned out

235
00:13:16,170 --> 00:13:19,710
to be wrong. What they could not do was check it.

236
00:13:20,130 --> 00:13:21,430
To check it, they needed a picture.

237
00:13:22,890 --> 00:13:24,930
The board wrote one sentence about that position,

238
00:13:24,930 --> 00:13:26,830
which is the most important line in the report.

239
00:13:27,950 --> 00:13:32,350
The team was put in the untenable position of having to prove that a safety issue existed

240
00:13:32,350 --> 00:13:35,530
without the very images that would allow such a determination.

241
00:13:37,550 --> 00:13:38,930
Operations are supposed to be proved safe.

242
00:13:40,010 --> 00:13:42,250
NASA had turned the burden of proof upside down.

243
00:13:43,630 --> 00:13:45,190
People often say the crew never knew.

244
00:13:46,010 --> 00:13:49,870
That is not quite true. On the 23rd of January,

245
00:13:50,170 --> 00:13:52,270
a flight director emailed Husband and McCool,

246
00:13:52,710 --> 00:13:54,130
attached the video of the strike,

247
00:13:54,470 --> 00:13:57,330
and told them there was absolutely no concern for entry.

248
00:13:58,490 --> 00:14:00,730
The board recorded why that email was sent,

249
00:14:00,730 --> 00:14:03,590
not because anyone thought it was worthy of the crew's attention,

250
00:14:04,110 --> 00:14:07,130
because the crew might be asked about it in an upcoming media interview.

251
00:14:08,550 --> 00:14:10,310
Husband wrote back and thanked him.

252
00:14:11,730 --> 00:14:15,250
What they were never told was that three people had asked for a photograph of their wing

253
00:14:15,250 --> 00:14:17,370
and that the request had been called off.

254
00:14:18,790 --> 00:14:21,010
And there is one more, nine days later,

255
00:14:21,310 --> 00:14:22,390
that almost nobody tells.

256
00:14:23,710 --> 00:14:27,310
On the 29th of January, with Columbia still in orbit,

257
00:14:27,730 --> 00:14:29,270
the Defense Department came back,

258
00:14:29,270 --> 00:14:31,150
not with a question this time,

259
00:14:31,790 --> 00:14:37,330
with an offer. A NASA safety officer took it to the associate administrator for spaceflight.

260
00:14:37,850 --> 00:14:39,050
He said NASA would accept.

261
00:14:39,870 --> 00:14:43,630
But because the management team had already ruled this was not a safety of flight issue,

262
00:14:43,970 --> 00:14:47,190
he directed that the pictures be gathered only on a low priority,

263
00:14:47,570 --> 00:14:50,730
not to interfere basis. Ultimately,

264
00:14:51,090 --> 00:14:53,250
no imagery was taken. Three days later,

265
00:14:53,250 --> 00:14:56,070
they came home. Saturday morning.

266
00:14:57,290 --> 00:15:00,650
Entry interface over the Pacific at 44 minutes past eight.

267
00:15:01,530 --> 00:15:02,870
Columbia comes in over California,

268
00:15:03,450 --> 00:15:05,130
then Nevada, then New Mexico,

269
00:15:05,550 --> 00:15:10,590
trailing plasma. NASA later released the video the crew shot inside the cabin that

270
00:15:10,590 --> 00:15:12,670
morning. This is it. Suits on,

271
00:15:13,070 --> 00:15:14,750
visors up, going through the checklist.

272
00:15:16,470 --> 00:15:20,470
Laurel Clark is filming. You can hear them talking about how the plasma looks through

273
00:15:20,470 --> 00:15:25,270
the window. That orange glow is the air outside the window turning to plasma.

274
00:15:25,270 --> 00:15:28,390
At this point, everything is going exactly as it should.

275
00:15:29,810 --> 00:15:33,570
The recording ends here. It stops several minutes before anything goes wrong,

276
00:15:33,850 --> 00:15:37,090
and we stop with it. On the ground in Texas,

277
00:15:37,310 --> 00:15:40,730
people are filming it, because it is a nice thing to watch on a Saturday morning.

278
00:15:42,150 --> 00:15:47,350
At 854 and 24 seconds, the console officer responsible for the mechanical systems calls

279
00:15:47,350 --> 00:15:52,390
the flight director. I've just lost four separate temperature transducers on the

280
00:15:52,390 --> 00:15:53,290
left side of the vehicle,

281
00:15:53,750 --> 00:15:55,070
hydraulic return temperatures.

282
00:15:56,570 --> 00:15:59,530
You're telling me you lost them all at exactly the same time?

283
00:16:00,870 --> 00:16:03,430
All four of them are located in the aft part of the left wing,

284
00:16:03,990 --> 00:16:05,270
and there is no commonality.

285
00:16:06,790 --> 00:16:09,750
No commonality. The sensors are not wired together,

286
00:16:09,990 --> 00:16:12,030
so a single fault cannot explain it,

287
00:16:12,370 --> 00:16:14,330
which means something is happening to the left wing.

288
00:16:15,630 --> 00:16:17,630
Then the tire pressures on the left side go.

289
00:16:19,030 --> 00:16:22,410
Columbia, Houston, we see your tire pressure messages,

290
00:16:22,750 --> 00:16:24,350
and we did not copy your last call.

291
00:16:25,950 --> 00:16:28,750
The answer from the flight deck is one word and the beginning of another.

292
00:16:29,410 --> 00:16:30,670
The report prints it as Roger,

293
00:16:31,030 --> 00:16:32,830
and then, in square brackets,

294
00:16:33,230 --> 00:16:37,650
cut off in midword. 859 and 32 seconds,

295
00:16:38,130 --> 00:16:40,910
loss of signal, which, at that point in an entry,

296
00:16:41,190 --> 00:16:43,910
is completely normal. It happens on every flight.

297
00:16:44,950 --> 00:16:46,750
So nobody in that room reacts.

298
00:16:46,750 --> 00:16:49,270
They wait the way they always wait.

299
00:16:51,070 --> 00:16:53,650
46 seconds later, the vehicle comes apart over east Texas.

300
00:16:55,210 --> 00:16:58,790
Two pilots were training in an Apache helicopter out of Fort Hood that morning.

301
00:16:59,230 --> 00:17:00,650
Their targeting camera was running.

302
00:17:01,330 --> 00:17:02,150
This is what it recorded.

303
00:17:03,670 --> 00:17:05,030
One object becomes several,

304
00:17:05,490 --> 00:17:08,750
then more. Mission control has no idea.

305
00:17:09,250 --> 00:17:11,390
They are still working the tire pressure problem.

306
00:17:12,750 --> 00:17:17,090
For 12 more minutes, the loop carries an ordinary technical conversation about instrumentation.

307
00:17:18,410 --> 00:17:19,970
At about 12 minutes past 9,

308
00:17:20,290 --> 00:17:23,850
someone on the flight control team gets a call on his mobile phone from a person who

309
00:17:23,850 --> 00:17:25,150
has just seen it on television.

310
00:17:25,750 --> 00:17:28,210
He walks to the flight director's console and tells him.

311
00:17:29,450 --> 00:17:31,730
The flight director calls for the ground control officer.

312
00:17:32,170 --> 00:17:34,210
He has to call twice, lock the doors.

313
00:17:35,670 --> 00:17:36,890
No phone calls off -site,

314
00:17:37,250 --> 00:17:40,970
no data in or out. Every console begins saving its own data,

315
00:17:41,170 --> 00:17:42,310
because from that moment,

316
00:17:42,430 --> 00:17:45,450
the room is evidence. What followed?

317
00:17:47,150 --> 00:17:51,490
More than 25 ,000 people searched 2 .3 million acres of Texas and Louisiana,

318
00:17:52,110 --> 00:17:54,010
an area approaching the size of Connecticut.

319
00:17:55,630 --> 00:17:57,730
Satellites, infrared, radar,

320
00:17:57,910 --> 00:17:59,690
and high -altitude aircraft were all used.

321
00:18:00,390 --> 00:18:02,690
The board's assessment is that they proved of little value.

322
00:18:03,210 --> 00:18:05,470
It came down to people walking in a line through the woods.

323
00:18:06,910 --> 00:18:11,590
On the 27th of March, a helicopter searching the Angelina National Forest suffered a

324
00:18:11,590 --> 00:18:14,050
mechanical failure and went down at treetop height.

325
00:18:14,470 --> 00:18:15,950
It was too low to autorotate.

326
00:18:17,470 --> 00:18:19,210
Jules Meir, a contract pilot,

327
00:18:19,690 --> 00:18:23,110
and Charles Krennek of the Texas Forest Service were killed.

328
00:18:23,710 --> 00:18:28,550
Three others were hurt. The accident board put both of their names on the dedication

329
00:18:28,550 --> 00:18:32,610
page of its report with their job titles underneath the seven astronauts.

330
00:18:34,190 --> 00:18:36,030
Nine people died because of this accident,

331
00:18:36,210 --> 00:18:38,130
and the report says so on its first page.

332
00:18:39,350 --> 00:18:44,270
In July, engineers in San Antonio fired a block of that foam at a shuttle wing out of

333
00:18:44,270 --> 00:18:47,170
a nitrogen cannon at 530 miles an hour.

334
00:18:47,470 --> 00:18:49,930
It punched a hole 16 inches across,

335
00:18:50,310 --> 00:18:55,050
straight through. They did not fire it to find out whether foam could break a wing.

336
00:18:55,790 --> 00:18:57,250
The analysis already said it could.

337
00:18:57,890 --> 00:19:00,650
They fired it to end an argument inside their own organization.

338
00:19:01,790 --> 00:19:03,850
The board's conclusion is one sentence.

339
00:19:04,690 --> 00:19:08,470
NASA's organizational culture and structure had as much to do with this accident as the

340
00:19:08,470 --> 00:19:13,430
foam did. The sociologist who worked on this report has a name for what happened,

341
00:19:13,850 --> 00:19:15,430
the normalization of deviance.

342
00:19:16,910 --> 00:19:18,470
You see the thing that should not happen,

343
00:19:18,790 --> 00:19:19,990
and it does not kill you,

344
00:19:20,170 --> 00:19:22,110
so it stops being the thing that should not happen.

345
00:19:23,630 --> 00:19:28,750
In 1996, Diane Vaughn published a book arguing that Challenger was not caused by villains,

346
00:19:29,070 --> 00:19:32,730
but by an organization slowly agreeing that a dangerous thing was normal.

347
00:19:33,770 --> 00:19:37,610
In April 2003, she was called to testify before the Columbia board.

348
00:19:38,410 --> 00:19:39,730
Then they hired her onto the staff.

349
00:19:40,450 --> 00:19:42,150
Then she wrote Chapter 8 of the report.

350
00:19:43,270 --> 00:19:45,550
The chapter is called History as Cause.

351
00:19:46,150 --> 00:19:49,690
The woman who diagnosed the first accident was brought in to write the finding that nobody

352
00:19:49,690 --> 00:19:54,730
had listened. Her chapter says the causes of the institutional failure responsible

353
00:19:54,730 --> 00:19:56,550
for Challenger have not been fixed,

354
00:19:56,870 --> 00:19:59,030
and that, if they are not resolved,

355
00:19:59,430 --> 00:20:01,090
the scene is set for another accident.

356
00:20:02,390 --> 00:20:07,590
Sally Ride sat on the Challenger Commission in 1986 and on the Columbia board in 2003.

357
00:20:08,330 --> 00:20:10,210
She is the only person who ever did both.

358
00:20:11,730 --> 00:20:15,610
And the closest parallel the report draws between the two accidents is one sentence.

359
00:20:16,430 --> 00:20:18,950
Challenger's astronauts were told the cold was not a problem.

360
00:20:19,750 --> 00:20:22,210
Columbia's astronauts were told the foam was not a problem.

361
00:20:23,650 --> 00:20:26,090
Columbia itself is still at the Kennedy Space Center,

362
00:20:26,690 --> 00:20:31,070
84 ,000 pieces of it, in a room on the 16th floor of the building where they used to

363
00:20:31,070 --> 00:20:33,810
assemble it. And they lend it out.

364
00:20:34,670 --> 00:20:37,170
Researchers studying how spacecraft come apart can borrow pieces.

365
00:20:37,610 --> 00:20:40,870
It is the only destroyed spacecraft in history that is still working.

366
00:20:41,910 --> 00:20:43,970
The board made 29 recommendations.

367
00:20:44,890 --> 00:20:46,990
15 had to be done before anyone flew again.

368
00:20:48,270 --> 00:20:52,150
In July 2005, Discovery flew the return to flight mission.

369
00:20:52,470 --> 00:20:53,670
The tank shed foam again.

370
00:20:54,010 --> 00:20:59,010
It missed. NASA grounded the fleet the following day and did not fly again for

371
00:20:59,010 --> 00:21:02,450
11 months. Five years after the accident,

372
00:21:02,530 --> 00:21:06,770
a second report looked at the crew module itself and at what could and could not have

373
00:21:06,770 --> 00:21:11,410
been survived. Its conclusion was that the breakup and what followed was not survivable

374
00:21:11,410 --> 00:21:13,050
by any capability that existed.

375
00:21:14,130 --> 00:21:17,870
It also found that even a better equipped cabin would not have changed it.

376
00:21:19,150 --> 00:21:21,690
One last thing. In July 2001,

377
00:21:22,090 --> 00:21:26,090
a survey at Palomar picked up seven small asteroids between Mars and Jupiter.

378
00:21:27,130 --> 00:21:30,510
In August 2003, they were named Rick Husband,

379
00:21:30,930 --> 00:21:32,730
Michael Anderson, David Brown,

380
00:21:33,310 --> 00:21:34,990
Kalpana Chala, Laurel Clark,

381
00:21:35,470 --> 00:21:37,130
Elan Ramon, William McCool.

382
00:21:38,550 --> 00:21:41,390
Those seven rocks were found 18 months before the crew died.

383
00:21:42,130 --> 00:21:43,250
They were already out there,

384
00:21:43,490 --> 00:21:46,090
unnamed, while all seven were alive and training.

385
00:21:47,450 --> 00:21:51,350
If the part that stays with you is how an organization talks itself past a warning,

386
00:21:51,790 --> 00:21:54,410
we made the whole story of everyone who never came back.

387
00:21:54,410 --> 00:21:56,990
21 astronauts, every one of them,

388
00:21:57,210 --> 00:21:58,330
right here on this channel.

